## Solution

We determine all diffeomorphisms $f:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^n$ preserving the Euclidean (flat) connection $\nabla$, i.e. satisfying
$$f_*(\nabla_X Y)=\nabla_{f_*X}\,f_*Y \quad\text{for all vector fields }X,Y.$$

**Claim.** The solutions are exactly the affine maps $f(x)=Ax+b$ with $A\in GL(n,\mathbb{R})$ and $b\in\mathbb{R}^n$.

---

### Coordinate computation

Work in standard coordinates $(x^1,\dots,x^n)$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$, where the Euclidean connection has vanishing Christoffel symbols, so
$$\nabla_X Y = X^i\,\partial_i Y^j\,\partial_j.$$

Let $J = Df$ denote the Jacobian of $f$, with entries $J^l{}_j = \partial f^l/\partial x^j$. Since $f$ is a diffeomorphism, $J$ is everywhere invertible.

**Left-hand side.** At $q=f(p)$:
$$(f_*(\nabla_X Y))^l\big|_{q} = J^l{}_j(p)\,X^i(p)\,\partial_i Y^j(p). \tag{1}$$

**Right-hand side.** The pushed-forward fields are $(f_*X)^k(q)=J^k{}_i(p)\,X^i(p)$ and $(f_*Y)^l(q)=J^l{}_j(p)\,Y^j(p)$, where $p=f^{-1}(q)$. Using the chain rule $\partial/\partial q^k = (J^{-1})^m{}_k\,\partial/\partial p^m$:

$$\partial_k(f_*Y)^l\Big|_{q=f(p)} = (J^{-1})^m{}_k\big(\partial_m J^l{}_j\cdot Y^j + J^l{}_j\,\partial_m Y^j\big)\Big|_p.$$

Therefore
$$(\nabla_{f_*X}f_*Y)^l\Big|_{q=f(p)} = J^k{}_i\,X^i\cdot(J^{-1})^m{}_k\big(\partial_m J^l{}_j\cdot Y^j + J^l{}_j\,\partial_m Y^j\big)$$
$$= \delta^m_i\,X^i\big(\partial_m J^l{}_j\cdot Y^j + J^l{}_j\,\partial_m Y^j\big) = X^i(\partial_i J^l{}_j)Y^j + J^l{}_j\,X^i\partial_i Y^j. \tag{2}$$

### Equating (1) and (2)

The common term $J^l{}_j\,X^i\partial_i Y^j$ cancels, leaving
$$X^i\,(\partial_i J^l{}_j)\,Y^j = 0 \quad\text{for all vector fields }X,Y\text{ and all }l.$$

Choosing $X=\partial_a$, $Y=\partial_b$ (coordinate fields) gives $\partial_a J^l{}_b=0$ for every $a,b,l$. Hence **$J=Df$ is constant**.

### Conclusion

A map with constant Jacobian has the form $f(x)=Ax+b$ with $A$ a constant matrix and $b$ a constant vector. Since $f$ is a diffeomorphism, $A$ must be invertible, i.e. $A\in GL(n,\mathbb{R})$.

**Verification.** For $f(x)=Ax+b$: the Jacobian is the constant $A$, so $f_*X = AX$ and $f_*Y=AY$. Then
$$f_*(\nabla_X Y) = A\big(X^i\partial_i Y^j\,\partial_j\big) = (AX)^i\,\partial_i(AY)^j\,\partial_j = \nabla_{AX}(AY)=\nabla_{f_*X}f_*Y,$$
since $A$ is constant so $\partial_i(AY)^j = A^j{}_k\,\partial_i Y^k$. ✓

---

**General form.** The diffeomorphisms $f:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^n$ preserving the Euclidean connection are precisely the **affine transformations**
$$\boxed{f(x) = Ax + b, \quad A\in GL(n,\mathbb{R}),\; b\in\mathbb{R}^n.}$$

